digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) In recent years, there has been an explosion in the discovery of bacterial phage defense systems."];
  "N10" [label="(0,0,0) If enzymatic counter-defenses are harder to characterize, then investigating them could reveal novel mechanisms."];
  "N17" [label="(13,0,0) DarTG1 systems have DarG1 antitoxin from the NADAR superfamily, while DarTG2 systems have DarG2 antitoxin with a macrodomain."];
  "N19" [label="(0,0,0) If antitoxins reverse ADP-ribosylation with specificity, then they function as enzymatic erasers of the toxin's modification."];
  "N22" [label="(19,0,0) SECϕ18 phage acquires resistance through DNA polymerase mutations enabling replication of ADP-ribosylated DNA."];
  "N23" [label="(20,0,0) AdfA and AdfB are anti-DarTG counter-defenses identified in phages RB69 and ICP1 through escape mutant analyses."];
  "N24" [label="(22,0,0) AdfA and AdfB are small proteins with no homology to known families that block DarT activity through direct interaction with the toxin."];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple independent phages evolved different counter-strategies, then counter-defense evolution is diverse and ongoing."];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Multiple phages evolved different counter-strategies, and this pattern indicates diverse ongoing evolution. Therefore, phage counter-defense against DarTG is evolutionarily diverse and active."];
  "N27" [label="(24,0,0) T4 phage encodes an active DarT1-blocking AdfA allele, but T4 ΔadfA phage remains resistant to DarTG1."];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) If a phage remains resistant after removing a known counter-defense, then an additional unknown counter-defense mechanism must exist."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) If both defense systems and counter-defense mechanisms are being discovered rapidly, then there is an ongoing evolutionary arms race between bacteria and phages."];
  "N31" [label="(27,0,0) Neither the presence of AdfA nor its allele type correlates with DarTG1 susceptibility in Tevenvirinae phages."];
  "N33" [label="(0,0,0) If AdfA presence does not correlate with resistance but nearly all Tevenvirinae are resistant, then a different conserved factor must confer resistance."];
  "N36" [label="(30,0,0) This element, termed anti-DarT factor NADAR (AdfN), is both necessary and sufficient for phage counter-defense."];
  "N37" [label="(0,0,0) If a genetic element is both necessary and sufficient for resistance, then it is the primary determinant of the resistance phenotype."];
  "N39" [label="(31,0,0) AdfN T4 is a member of the NADAR superfamily and has enzymatic activity that removes ADP-ribose from DNA, like DarG1 antitoxin."];
  "N41" [label="(0,0,0) If AdfN has the same enzymatic activity as DarG1 antitoxin (removing ADP-ribose from DNA), then AdfN functions by reversing DarT1-mediated DNA modification."];
  "N43" [label="(33,0,0) Phylogenetic analyses indicate phages independently co-opted NADAR domain proteins multiple times from bacterial or archaeal sources."];
  "N44" [label="(33,0,0) These NADAR proteins function as orphan antitoxins to reverse the activity of TA-associated DarT1 toxin."];
  "N45" [label="(0,0,0) If phages independently acquired the same functional domain multiple times, then this represents convergent evolution driven by strong selective pressure."];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Phages independently acquired NADAR domains multiple times, and this pattern indicates convergent evolution under strong selection. Therefore, DarTG1 defense exerts strong selective pressure driving convergent evolution of NADAR-based counter-defenses in phages."];
  "N47" [label="(0,0,0) If enzymatic counter-defenses are underrepresented in current knowledge, then discovering enzymatic mechanisms like AdfN addresses a knowledge gap."];
  "N5" [label="(3,0,0) Most phage counter-defense mechanisms described to date are non-enzymatic in nature."];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) If T4 and relatives possess a conserved enzymatic NADAR-based counter-defense (AdfN) that reverses DarT1 activity, and this represents an underexplored class of counter-defenses, then characterizing AdfN's mechanism and evolution will reveal novel insights into enzymatic phage counter-defense strategies and the evolutionary arms race between phages and bacterial TA systems."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Relatively fewer enzymatic counter-defense proteins have been identified compared to non-enzymatic ones."];
  "N7" [label="(0,0,0) If most known counter-defenses are non-enzymatic and fewer enzymatic ones have been identified, then there is a general pattern of non-enzymatic dominance in characterized counter-defenses."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-reasoning: Most counter-defenses are non-enzymatic and fewer enzymatic ones are identified, and this pattern suggests non-enzymatic dominance. Therefore, non-enzymatic counter-defenses dominate the characterized landscape."];
  "N9" [label="(8,0,0) It is unclear whether enzymatic counter-defenses are less common or just more challenging to characterize."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to investigate how Tevenvirinae phages evade DarTG1 bacterial defense by characterizing AdfN, a conserved NADAR-domain protein and orphan antitoxin, as a phage counter-defense that enzymatically removes DarT1-installed ADP-ribose from DNA and illustrates independent phage co-option of NADAR proteins to neutralize DarT toxins.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When phage resistance to a bacterial toxin-antitoxin defense is associated with a conserved enzymatic factor that reverses the toxin's DNA modification, the factor is a strong target for mechanistic and evolutionary characterization."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) AdfN is a primary determinant of phage counter-defense against DarTG1 because it is necessary and sufficient for the resistance phenotype."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) AdfN functions as an enzymatic counter-defense by removing DarT1-installed ADP-ribose from DNA and thereby reversing DarT1-mediated DNA modification."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Characterizing AdfN as an enzymatic counter-defense addresses an underrepresented area in current knowledge of phage counter-defense diversity."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The study is motivated to characterize AdfN as a conserved NADAR-based counter-defense underlying T4 and Tevenvirinae resistance to DarTG1."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Rapid discovery of both bacterial phage defense systems and phage counter-defense mechanisms supports framing bacteria-phage interactions as an ongoing evolutionary arms race."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Uncertainty over whether enzymatic counter-defenses are rare or difficult to characterize motivates investigating them as a route to finding additional mechanisms."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) DarG antitoxins function as specific enzymatic erasers of DarT-mediated DNA ADP-ribosylation."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because T4 ΔadfA remains resistant to DarTG1, T4 likely carries an additional anti-DarTG1 counter-defense mechanism besides AdfA."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The broad DarTG1 resistance of Tevenvirinae phages, despite poor correlation with AdfA, supports the existence of a different conserved resistance factor."];
  "N5" -> "N8" [label="induction-case"];
